      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
        Standard Volumetric Preparation Workflow
      </h3>
      <ol style="margin-left: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem;">
        <li><strong>Analytical Balance Weighing:</strong> Tare a clean weighing boat and weigh the gross mass on a calibrated analytical balance (&plusmn;0.0001 g precision).</li>
        <li><strong>Initial Beaker Dissolution:</strong> Transfer the salt into a clean glass beaker containing ~60% of the target deionized water volume. Stir gently until solid dissolution is complete.</li>
        <li><strong>Thermal Equilibrate:</strong> Allow any endothermic cooling or exothermic heat of solution to return to ambient laboratory temperature (20&deg;C).</li>
        <li><strong>Quantitative Transfer:</strong> Pour the solution into a Class-A volumetric flask using a clean funnel. Rinse the beaker walls and funnel 3 times with solvent aliquots into the flask.</li>
        <li><strong>Meniscus Adjustment:</strong> Dilute dropwise until the lowest curved boundary of the meniscus touches the graduation line at eye level. Stopper and invert 10 times.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- BENCHTOP TROUBLESHOOTING GUIDE -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        Benchtop Analytical Troubleshooting Matrix
      </h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        Common preparation discrepancies encountered during volumetric weighing and their analytical resolutions:
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #b91c1c; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Exothermic Dissolution Error (e.g. NaOH)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Dissolving concentrated bases produces massive exothermic dissolution enthalpy (&Delta;H<sub>soln</sub> = &minus;44.5 kJ/mol). Diluting to the calibration line while hot causes the solution to contract upon cooling, resulting in a significantly over-concentrated standard.<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Dissolve pellets in a beaker on an ice bath or let equilibrate to 20°C before transferring to a Class-A volumetric flask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #d97706; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Deliquescent &amp; Hygroscopic Mass Drift
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Salts like anhydrous CaCl₂, LiCl, and KOH rapidly absorb moisture from ambient air while on the weighing balance, causing balance readings to continuously drift upward.<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Use weighing by difference with a stoppered weighing bottle, or select stable hydrated crystalline forms (e.g., CaCl₂·2H₂O) using the integrated hydrate selector.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #0284c7; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Carbonate Contamination in Hydroxide Stocks
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Aqueous NaOH absorbs atmospheric CO₂ to form sodium carbonate (2 NaOH + CO₂ &rarr; Na₂CO₃ + H₂O), altering titrimetric inflection points.<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Prepare a 50% w/w NaOH stock where Na₂CO₃ is insoluble and precipitates out; decant clear supernatant using boiled, degassed Milli-Q water.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Solution Molarity &amp; Analytical Weighing
      </h3>
      
      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the physical difference between molarity and molality?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Molarity (M) denotes moles of solute per liter of total solution, which is temperature-dependent due to solvent thermal expansion. Molality (m) represents moles of solute per kilogram of pure solvent, which remains strictly independent of temperature.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why must chemical assay purity be factored into molarity calculations?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Manufacturer reagents are rarely 100% pure due to residual moisture and trace synthetic precursors. If an assay reads 96.0%, weighing 100 g supplies only 96 g of active solute, yielding an under-concentrated standard unless compensated.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why should solutions never be dissolved directly inside a volumetric flask?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Salts often display significant dissolution heats (&Delta;H<sub>solution</sub>). Volumetric flasks are calibrated strictly at 20&deg;C; dissolving hot or cold reagents alters glass dimensions and causes inaccurate volume markings before thermal equilibrium.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What are the ASTM Class-A volumetric flask tolerances?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Per ASTM E288 and ISO 1042 standards, Class-A flasks have strictly enforced volume limits: 100 mL flasks carry a tolerance of &plusmn;0.08 mL, 250 mL flasks have &plusmn;0.12 mL, 500 mL flasks have &plusmn;0.20 mL, and 1000 mL flasks have &plusmn;0.30 mL.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why must primary standards such as KHP and Na2CO3 be oven-dried prior to weighing?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Primary standards pick up surface-adsorbed atmospheric moisture during storage. Oven drying (e.g., KHP at 110°C–120°C for 2 hours, or anhydrous Na₂CO₃ at 260°C–270°C for 1 hour) drives off free moisture so that analytical balance readings reflect 100.0% pure stoichiometric mass.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

    </article>
  </main>

  <style>
    .molarity-preset-btn {
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1;
      padding: 0.35rem 0.65rem;
